## Lore

The Shadow People are a kind of Australian faerie folk: tall, dusky grey
and astonishingly thin, with a waist no thicker than a man''s forearm, arms
nearly as long as the body, a long tapering face and large, drooping, dark
eyes. They look sad until they smile. Parts of them vanish in shadow, and
in darkness they disappear altogether. The Aborigines have always known
them and painted them on cave walls; the two peoples shared a quiet
friendship long before white settlers came. The Shadow People slept in the
rocks through the ages when magic was low and woke with the Great
Cataclysm, and the tribes greeted them as old friends.

They are gentle, open-minded and painfully shy, yet too curious to stay
hidden: they creep through rocks and hills to watch the world from the
shade. Families of three to seven live inside cracks in boulders and
hillsides, where their magic opens a small pocket dimension, a cave lit by
a sourceless orange glow and covered in paintings. They eat only plants,
with beetles, ants and grubs as treats.

Their bodies are too slight for a slugging match, so they fight with
magic, stealth, traps, ambush and scare tactics, and are usually content to
drive a threat away. A player character is likely young and unusually
bold, or else a nervous soul who has joined a group out of duty or
friendship. Either will never abandon a friend, and both are uneasy out on
open ground where there is nowhere to hide.

## GM Notes

The book heads the race an NPC or optional player character, allowed if
the G.M. agrees.

Conditional bonuses the sheet does not add: +3 on initiative, +1 more to
dodge and +15% to prowl at night or in the dark.

In place of the six wizard spells of levels 1-4, a Shadow Person may take
three Temporal Magic spells of levels 7-9 (Rifts England).

Vulnerability to magic: on a Songline, ley line or other place of magic
they lose their shadow and rock powers and cast at half strength; magic,
magic weapons and the physical attacks of creatures of magic do double
damage; a Ley Line Storm within 100 miles shuts their powers off.

Their native language is a strange dialect of Faerie Speak (-20% to those
who speak Faerie); the catalog has no row for it.

Horror Factor: none, though seeing one fade in and out of shadow is
unnerving. Size at least 6 feet plus 2D6+1 inches (1.91 to 2.16 m); weight
only 50-75 lbs. Life span unknown: at least a thousand years, perhaps ten
times that, and they hibernate without aging while magic is low. NPCs
average level 1D4+3.

Habitat is large rocks, craggy hills, canyons, bluffs and mountains in the
drier north, mostly the Rim territories; never within 200 miles (320 km) of
a Tech-City. Enemies are whoever threatens the Millennium Trees or the
Dreamtime. Allies are the Aboriginal tribes and some Outbackers, and they
lean favorably toward the Kwarla. Nobody knows how many there are; the
Aboriginals guess fewer than 10,000.

The block prints no starting equipment and no money.
